Debian 13 update (2026-10-01)
----------------------------
See QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13 for the native amd64/arm64 package qualification.
Ubuntu-only scope statements below describe the initial packaging checkpoint.

Qore NATS module packages
========================

This is a development snapshot for qore-testing, not a final release.
The runtime contains the native NATS bindings, three source/AOT user modules,
compiler metadata, provider resources and all translation catalogs. The doc
package contains four API references and test fixtures. No broker is installed
or started by the runtime package.

Build with dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc and the matching Qore 3 SDK. Distribution
libnats-dev >= 3.12.0 is required; no build-time downloads or private client
library are used. Upstream nats.c 3.12 already fixes microservice error counts;
the obsolete FetchContent string patch is removed. The microservice suite
checks both successful requests and errors against the distribution library.

Full, arch-only, indep-only, nocheck and nodoc profiles are supported. Fixed
qcc partitioning, declared sibling-module dependencies and preserved AOT
trailers support deterministic builds and installed compilation. Cross builds
need a separate host SDK and are rejected.

Build and installed tests start local loopback nats-server processes; they
exercise messaging, JetStream, key-value stores, microservices and providers.
Consumer-pause tests skip explicitly with broker versions that lack that API;
qualify those cases separately with a newer broker.
The NATS CLI interoperability suite reports an explicit QUnit skip if the CLI
is absent (it is not supplied by the distribution). Qualify it separately
before claiming cross-client coverage. Installed compiler tests compile and
run a fresh messaging client; fixture module paths exclude the checkout.
No external account is required. TLS/authentication deployment combinations
and multi-node broker clusters require separate qualification.

Before general publication, qualify other Debian/Ubuntu releases and
architectures, freeze the Qore ABI and test relevant upgrades. No older module
package is available for upgrade testing. PPA publication requires the matching
Qore SDK, source signing and upload.
